/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape05_thermal_no
  ======================

  THE THERMAL-NO LANDSCAPE -- CH4 in stoichiometric air with NO in the
  species list (gibbs06_thermal_nox's own feed and species set), the
  equilibrium NO mole fraction against temperature at 1, 10 and 100 bar,
  1000 to 2500 K.  The page draws it on a LOG axis: it spans many decades (read the two ends off the CSV rather than off this sentence).

  WHAT IT IS HERE FOR, and it is the sharpest thing on the page.  NO
  formation (N2 + O2 -> 2 NO) is ENDOTHERMIC -- the curve RISES with T --
  but it rides inside a feed whose OVERALL transformation, methane
  burning, is exothermic.  The engine reads the direction from the overall
  thermicity of THIS feed at each cell (GibbsReactor::approachDirection,
  the one home; GibbsMapOp calls it per cell), finds every cell exothermic,
  and evaluates the chemistry at T + dT.  For the combustion that is the
  conservative direction.  For the NO riding inside it, T + dT sits HIGHER
  on a rising curve: the declared approach reports MORE NO than the
  equilibrium at the physical T -- an outlet beyond equilibrium for the
  minor product.  That is the engine's own GLOBAL caveat ("it cannot
  resolve per-reaction approaches") drawn as a curve rather than printed
  as a sentence, and the page says it is a limitation of the single-number
  model, not a finding about flames.

  Pressure: 2 -> 2 for NO formation, so the 1 and 10 bar curves nearly
  coincide; the small spread that remains comes from the combustion
  products' own dissociation equilibria, which do carry P.

  WHY THE PRESSURES ARE 1, 10 AND 100 BAR.  `Pgrid` is `from / to / n / log`
  (GibbsMapOp.cpp reads no list form), and three log-spaced points from 1 to
  100 bar land on the decades a textbook draws.  Ideal-gas validity is the
  same caveat as nh3_equilibrium_map's: the high-pressure curve is the
  SHAPE of the pressure dependence, not a quantitative prediction.

  temperatureApproach IS DECLARED, AT ZERO.  The EduTool's slider replaces
  the NUMBER of a declared scalar and can add no key (gui/src/case/
  methodRun.ts), so the key must be here for the page to move it.  Zero is
  true equilibrium and prints nothing; a magnitude > 0 makes the run assign
  the sign at EVERY cell from the thermicity of the overall transformation
  at that cell's T (GibbsMapOp.cpp, `resolveDirection`) and print the tally.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      burner;
        type      gibbsMap;
        elements  ( C H O N );
        species
        (
            { name CH4;   atoms ( 1 4 0 0 ); }
            { name O2;    atoms ( 0 0 2 0 ); }
            { name N2;    atoms ( 0 0 0 2 ); }
            { name CO;    atoms ( 1 0 1 0 ); }
            { name CO2;   atoms ( 1 0 2 0 ); }
            { name water; atoms ( 0 2 1 0 ); }
            { name H2;    atoms ( 0 2 0 0 ); }
            { name NO;    atoms ( 0 0 1 1 ); }
        );
        feed      { CH4 1.000452;  O2 1.999852;  N2 7.519696; }
        Tgrid     { from 1000; to 2500; n 31; }             // K
        Pgrid     { from 1.0e5; to 1.0e7; n 3; log true; }  // Pa: 1, 10, 100 bar
        temperatureApproach 0;                          // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        metric    { type moleFraction; species NO; }
        anchors
        (
            { T 1800;     P 1.0e5;   }   // gibbs06_thermal_nox's operating point
        );
        output    { file map.csv; }
    }
);
